<p>Collaborative annotations are graded for completion: you will get full credit for them as long as you submit the required number of annotations and comments (three of each) and they reflect a reasonable and good-faith attempt to engage with the assigned texts and your classmates' comments.</p>

<h4>Three short essays</h4>
<p>You will write three short essays (800-1000 words) over the course of the semester. These essays provide you with the opportunity to practice your writing skills at home. They draw on ideas and concepts from class and the readings that you will have engaged with in the prior weeks and you are expected to develop your own argument in response to them. Each essay will be based on a prompt that will be released on Canvas ahead of time. They are due on the dates specified in the course schedule.</p>

<h4>Three group tutorials (45 minutes each)</h4>
Three group tutorials (45 minutes each): these are based on your short essays. You will be assigned to a tutorial group (of 3-4 students) which will meet three times with the instructor throughout the semester. In preparation for the tutorial, you will read and comment on each other’s short essays. Those comments and any questions that arise from your prior engagement will form the basis of a 45 minute group discussion in which you will provide and receive critical feedback on your and your groupmates’ arguments and writing, and deepen your understanding of the material. This is also the place where you can ask questions that go beyond that material and dig even deeper.

<h4>Two short in-class paper-based exams (40 minutes each)</h4>
Those exams will consist of a very short argumentative writing task and some brief questions that test your understanding of some key ideas and concepts we have discussed in class in the previous weeks. The take-home essays and tutorials will prepare you for the tasks you will encounter in the exams.

<h4>A choice of EITHER a final paper/project OR a longer final exam</h4>
<h5>Final paper/project </h5>
This is a long-form assignment in which you will be able to exercise your philosophical reasoning and writing skills. This project is on a topic of your choice (subject to instructor approval) related to the course material. You have to meet with Julia at least twice prior to handing in your final project to receive guidance and feedback. You will also meet with Julia once more after you have handed in your project for a 30 minute tutorial to discuss and receive feedback. Both the written part of the project and the tutorial are part of your final project grade.

<h5>Final exam</h5>
This is a 120 minute paper-based final exam which consists of both short argumentative tasks and questions to test your understanding of key ideas and concepts you will have encountered throughout the semester. 

IMPORTANT: If you would like to select the final paper/project option, you must notify Julia of that choice by October 31st. (A form will be distributed in advance.) If you do not make a choice, you will be automatically enrolled in the final exam. You may switch to the final exam option at any time before the final paper/project is due, but you may not switch to the final paper/project option after October 31st.


<h3>Grading scheme</h3>

<p>Grade inflation is real, and everyone in college has to contend with it.</p>

<p>One of the downsides of grade inflation is that grades lose their communicative value. Let me explain: we often tend to see the purpose of grades as summative assessment – providing information on how well you did in the class to external audiences, e.g., the university, other institutions, or future employers.</p>

<p>However, grades can (and should) also be a tool of formative assessment – providing you and me as an instructor with an honest indication of where your strengths and weaknesses lie, what you need to work on, and giving both of us an idea of your skill-building trajectory across the course. This signal gets lost if almost everyone expects and receives an A for each component of the course.</p>

<p>At the same time, grading in any one single course cannot operate in isolation of the expectations the rest of the world has about the meaning of a grade. If a B is perceived as a "bad" grade by others, it doesn't matter that, within the confines of a course, it's meant to reflect a good effort which merely lacks some features that make it fall short of the "outstanding" mastery of skills required for an A.</p>

<p>In this course, I want to be cognizant of all of these dimensions. Hence, we will operate two grading schemes:</p>

<ol>
  <li>a formative, honest grading scheme that is finely differentiated and aims to truly reflect your trajectory of skill development and provides honest feedback; and</li>
  <li>a summative grading scheme in which the more differentiated grades of the formative scheme will be translated into the standard ABC/NC or S/NC grading scheme.</li>
</ol>

<p>Here's what this will look like in practice:</p>

<p>The grades you receive on all assignments (including exams) will follow a numerical scheme from 1-6. Here's what the numbers mean:</p>

<ul>
  <li>1: outstanding</li>
  <li>2: good</li>
  <li>3: satisfactory</li>
  <li>4: sufficient: B</li>
  <li>5: deficient: C</li>
  <li>6: insufficient: NC</li>
</ul>

<p>For summative grading and your final grade at the end of the semester, I will translate your grades as follows.</p>

<p><b>Letter grade translation:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Grades 1-3 will be translated to an A;</li>
  <li>Grade 4 is equivalent to a B;</li>
  <li>Grade 5 to a C;</li>
  <li>and grade 6 is a failing grade.</li>
</ul>

<p><b>S/NC grade translation:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Grades 1-5 are equivalent to an S.</li>
  <li>Grade 6 is a failing grade.</li>
</ul>

<p>This means that final grades follow the same distribution as they historically have and do in other courses, meaning that external perception and comparison in a grade-inflated environment remains consistent. But it also means that you receive honest feedback throughout the semester, and you'll know how strong your work is and where you can improve even work that will ultimately result in an A.</p>

<p>You have my commitment that I will honor this translation: if your grades are in the 1-3 range, you will receive an A at the end of the semester (even if they are all 3s).</p>


