<h3>AI policy</h3>

<p><b>In short, all of the written work you hand in (this includes all collaborative annotations, essays and exams) must have been outlined, argued, and written by you without the use of AI tools.</b></p>

<p>This policy is not ignorant of the fact that AI tools are transforming the way in which we collect and process information, and communicate our ideas. They can help us collect and summarize information and perspectives, provide feedback and polish our writing. That's great!</p>

<p>But the point of taking a philosophy course is not to produce the most impressive-looking essay in the most efficient manner. It's to build true understanding and develop your capacity for critical reasoning and judgment. Here's the rub: developing those skills requires manual practice. That practice involves reasoning your way through a gnarly argument on your own, and also the struggle to put those ideas in your own words – writing is catalyzes our thought process.</p>

<p>Short-circuiting these tasks by using AI tools for the developing and conveying your arguments (i.e., reasoning, outlining and writing) will prevent you from learning what this course intends to teach you. That's why this policy does not permit the use of AI tools in the manner outlined above. Submitting work that is clearly copied from AI output also violates Brown's Academic Code and will be pursued as such.</p>

<p><b>You are explicitly welcome, though not required, to use AI tools for tasks that support you in building your argumentative skills: i.e., helping you understand ideas, concepts, and debates, and to find additional resources.</b> Keep in mind, though, that AI tools aren't always reliable, especially when it comes to providing information about contested or more niche ideas and topics. When you decide to use them for research, I strongly recommend you treat them as springboards for further research and argumentation to back up the information they provide.</p>

<p>In short, in order to be successful in the course and do well on all graded parts of the course (annotations, short essays and tutorials, short exams, the final paper or exam), you have to put in the legwork of practicing to think, argue and write yourself.</p>

<p>If you have any questions about this policy or are unsure about whether might be in violation of it, please get in touch before you start working on an assignment. I am happy to discuss this with you and provide guidance on how to use AI tools in a way that is consistent with the goals of the course and this policy.</p>


